<b>ד.</b> "One who rises early." The kabbalists greatly expanded on midnight prayer. Recanati (Bereshit) writes that some say the time for requesting mercy is at the end of the night, but he rules: at midnight one should seek mercy for Knesset Yisrael and her children, and at night's end request personal needs, with this request made in nefilat apayim (see siman 131). In our Talmud, according to the first answer that gave a sign for "the middle of the middle," i.e., midnight, it implies a time of favor; according to the second answer, not necessarily. See Zohar Vayakhel: night is always reckoned as twelve hours in both summer and winter, though for prayer times this is not so (siman 233). So too in Shaarei Tzion.
